Short answer: Yes. Adding ServoEasing, S-Curve (cubic/sigmoid), or cubic polynomial interpolation is exactly how you make servos move smoothly instead of with jerky, instantaneous jumps.

Below is a practical breakdown of the three approaches and code you can drop into Arduino (C++) or Python projects.

1. ServoEasing (Library Approach)

If you're on Arduino, the ServoEasing library is the easiest path. It handles easing curves (linear, quad, cubic, quartic, quintic, sine, expo, circ, back, elastic, bounce) and non-blocking movement.

Arduino Example (ServoEasing)


#include <ServoEasing.hpp>

ServoEasing myServo;
const int servoPin = 9;

void setup() {
  myServo.attach(servoPin);
  myServo.setSpeed(60);           // degrees per second
  myServo.write(90);              // start at center
}

void loop() {
  // Move smoothly to 160° with a default easing curve
  myServo.setEasingType(EASE_CUBIC_IN_OUT);
  myServo.easeTo(160, 300);       // target degrees, duration ms (or setSpeed handles it)
  delay(4000);
  
  myServo.easeTo(20, 300);
  delay(4000);
}
Key benefit: Non-blocking — your loop can keep reading sensors while the servo glides.

2. S-Curve / Cubic Polynomial (Custom Implementation)

If you're writing your own motion control (e.g., on ESP32, Raspberry Pi Pico, or a custom motor driver), a cubic polynomial (also called smoothstep or S-curve) is the standard way to generate velocity-limited, acceleration-limited position profiles.

The Math

A cubic S-curve eases from 0 → 1 over normalized time t (0 to 1):


smoothstep(t) = 3t² − 2t³        (basic S-curve)
smootherstep(t) = 6t⁵ − 15t⁴ + 10t³  (zero acceleration at endpoints)
For servo control, you interpolate between startPos and targetPos:


position(t) = startPos + (targetPos − startPos) * smootherstep(t / totalTime)
Arduino/C++ Example (Non-blocking)


class SmoothServo {
  public:
    SmoothServo(int pin) : _pin(pin), _moving(false) {}
    
    void attach() { _servo.attach(_pin); }
    
    // Start a smooth move to target degrees over durationMs
    void moveTo(float target, unsigned long durationMs) {
      _startPos = _servo.read();
      _targetPos = target;
      _startTime = millis();
      _duration = durationMs;
      _moving = true;
    }
    
    void update() {
      if (!_moving) return;
      
      unsigned long now = millis();
      unsigned long elapsed = now - _startTime;
      
      if (elapsed >= _duration) {
        _servo.write(_targetPos);
        _moving = false;
        return;
      }
      
      float t = (float)elapsed / (float)_duration;          // 0 → 1
      float s = smootherstep(t);                            // S-curve 0 → 1
      float pos = _startPos + (_targetPos - _startPos) * s;
      
      _servo.write(pos);
    }
    
    bool isMoving() { return _moving; }
    
  private:
    float smootherstep(float t) {
      return t * t * t * (t * (t * 6 - 15) + 10);  // 6t⁵ − 15t⁴ + 10t³
    }
    
    Servo _servo;
    int _pin;
    float _startPos, _targetPos;
    unsigned long _startTime, _duration;
    bool _moving;
};
