Yes. On the STM32F429ZI (Cortex‑M4F, up to 180 MHz) you should not use the Arduino ServoEasing library—it is written for AVR/SAMD and will not compile cleanly on Cortex‑M without heavy changes.

Instead, use a custom cubic S‑curve (smootherstep) written in plain C with STM32 HAL. The F429 has a hardware FPU, so floating‑point math is essentially free.

Below is a complete, copy‑pasteable example using TIM2 Channel 1 on PA0 to drive a standard hobby servo (50 Hz, 0.5 ms–2.5 ms pulse) with smooth, non‑blocking S‑curve motion.

Assumptions

Item	Value
MCU	STM32F429ZI
Clock	180 MHz (HSE 8 MHz, PLL)
Servo signal pin	PA0 → TIM2_CH1 (AF1)
PWM frequency	50 Hz (20 ms period)
Pulse range	500 µs (0°) → 2500 µs (180°)
Timer clock	90 MHz (APB1 ×2 because APB1 prescaler > 1)
Counter clock	1 MHz (1 tick = 1 µs)
If your board uses a different crystal or clock tree, adjust SystemClock_Config() and TIMER_CLK_HZ accordingly. The servo logic itself does not change.
