Why This Works Well on F429ZI

Feature	Benefit
Cortex‑M4F FPU	float math (smootherstep) runs in hardware—no slow software emulation.
32‑bit TIM2	Large period values (e.g., 19,999 ticks) fit easily; high resolution.
Non‑blocking	servo_update() uses HAL_GetTick(), so your main loop can read sensors, handle UART, etc. while the servo glides.
S‑curve	smootherstep has zero velocity and zero acceleration at both endpoints, eliminating the mechanical jerk of a linear ramp.
Making It Even Smoother (Timer Interrupt)

Calling servo_update() inside while(1) with HAL_Delay(10) works fine for most applications. If you want jitter‑free motion independent of main-loop latency, run the update from a basic timer interrupt:

Enable TIM6 (or TIM7) at 100 Hz (10 ms period).
In TIM6_DAC_IRQHandler, call servo_update(&my_servo);.
Remove HAL_Delay(10) from main().
Example IRQ:


void TIM6_DAC_IRQHandler(void)
{
    if (__HAL_TIM_GET_FLAG(&htim6, TIM_FLAG_UPDATE)) {
        __HAL_TIM_CLEAR_FLAG(&htim6, TIM_FLAG_UPDATE);
        servo_update(&my_servo);
    }
}
ServoEasing on STM32F429ZI?

Library	Works on F429ZI?	Notes
ServoEasing	❌ Not officially	Arduino/AVR‑centric; uses AVR timer registers (TCCR1A, etc.) that do not exist on Cortex‑M. Some forks claim STM32 support, but they are inconsistent.
Custom S‑curve (above)	✅ Native	HAL‑based, portable, full control over timing and curves.
Hardware Tips for STM32 + Servo

Power the servo separately. Do not power the servo from the STM32 3.3 V / 5 V rail. Use an external 5–6 V supply and connect grounds together.
Add decoupling. Place a 100 µF–470 µF electrolytic capacitor across the servo power rails near the servo.
Signal level. STM32 I/Os are 3.3 V. Most hobby servos recognize 3.3 V as a valid high signal, but if yours is erratic, add a simple level shifter or use a 74HCT125 to shift 3.3 V → 5 V.
