How the S-curve works in your stack


detection topic → C++ node (S-curve smoothing) → /arm_joint_commands
                                                  ↓
                                        serial bridge (optional S-curve)
                                                  ↓
                                           "J<n> <rad>\n" → STM32 → PCA9685 → Servos
C++ node: Each joint follows 6t⁵ − 15t⁴ + 10t³ (smootherstep). Velocity is zero at both start and end of every move, so the servo accelerates and decelerates gently — no snapping.
Serial bridge (optional): Adds a second S-curve layer right before the STM32, so even if another node sends abrupt commands, the servos still move smoothly.
If you want to fall back to the original EMA behavior, set use_scurve_smoothing:=false on the C++ node and enable_scurve:=false on the bridge.
